<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bangladesh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as People's Republic of Bangladesh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bangladesh, officially the People's Republic of Bangladesh, is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and Myanmar. Its territory was part of the Bengal Sultanate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> in 1947 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a>. After years of marginalisation by the government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-pakistan">West Pakistan</a>, Operation Searchlight began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a>, during which Pakistani forces carried out the Bangladesh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Bangladesh became sovereign on 16 December 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bangladesh.jpg" alt="Bangladesh" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bangladesh.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> (c. 320–185 BCE) was the first great indigenous empire to cover the territory. The kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samatata">Samatata</a> later arose as a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>, and the Buddhist Pala Empire ruled the region from 750 to 1150 CE until the Hindu Sena dynasty overthrew it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakhtiyar-khalji">Muhammad Bakhtiyar Khalji</a>'s conquests of 1204 brought Muslim rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. The independent Bengal Sultanate was founded in 1341 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fakhruddin-mubarak-shah">Fakhruddin Mubarak Shah</a>, and European and Chinese visitors regarded it as the richest country to trade with. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> conquered Bengal in 1576, and after its decline the Nawabs of Bengal, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murshid-quli-khan">Murshid Quli Khan</a> in 1717, ruled a semi-independent state.</p><p>On 23 June 1757 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> defeated Siraj-ud-Daulah at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a>, helped by the betrayal of his commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jafar">Mir Jafar</a>. In 1765 the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II granted the company diwani rights to collect revenue from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radcliffe-line">Radcliffe Line</a> partitioned Bengal on 15 August 1947, and the east became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a>, the most populous wing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, over 1,000 miles from the west. The Awami Muslim League was formed in 1949 and renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awami-league">Awami League</a> in 1953, and the United Front led by A. K. Fazlul Huq won the 1954 election in a landslide. East Bengal was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> in 1956.</p><p>The Constitution of Bangladesh was enacted on 4 November 1972. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a>'s government faced allegations of corruption and mismanagement, and a large famine in 1974 preceded his assassination in 1975. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziaur-rahman">Ziaur Rahman</a> founded the Bangladesh Nationalist Party on 1 September 1978 and was assassinated in 1981. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussain-muhammad-ershad">Hussain Muhammad Ershad</a> then led a military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> that declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the state religion in 1988.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bangladesh covers 148,461 square kilometres on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, most of it in the Ganges Delta. Dhaka is the capital and largest city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> the second city and busiest port. The 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 169.8 million people, in eight divisions. Bengali is the official language, spoken natively by more than 99 per cent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> is the state religion, although the constitution also upholds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secularism">secularism</a>. The Mosque City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagerhat">Bagerhat</a>, the Buddhist Vihara and the Sundarbans are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed Jamdani muslin weaving as intangible heritage in 2013.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bangladesh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>India, Pakistan, <b>Bangladesh</b> and Myanmar inherited its borders, provinces, armies, railways, legal codes and civil services; the Indian Administrative Service and the Indian Penal Code descend directly from colonial institutions, and English became a language of government, law and higher education across the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Four conventional wars have been fought with India: the Kashmir war of 1947–1948, which left Pakistan a third of the region; 1965; 1971, when India's support for the Bengali Mukti Bahini ended in Indian victory and the independence of <b>Bangladesh</b>; and the Kargil War of 1999.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It was among the first to recognise India's independence in 1947, the first to recognise <b>Bangladesh</b> in 1971, the year it joined the United Nations, and a founding member of SAARC in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bhutan">Kingdom of Bhutan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2017 army &quot;clearance operations&quot; in Rakhine State drove over 700,000 Rohingya into <b>Bangladesh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharmapala founded the monastic university of Vikramashila and the great monastery of Somapura at Paharpur, in present-day <b>Bangladesh</b>, and Devapala made grants to Nalanda, including five villages granted at the request of Balaputradeva, the Shailendra king of Suvarnadvipa (Sumatra), for a monastery he had built there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1971 India intervened in the Bangladesh Liberation War, and within two weeks some 93,000 Pakistani soldiers surrendered at Dhaka, creating <b>Bangladesh</b> and confirming India's military primacy in South Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Military</a></li></ul></section>
